NAME
       nmcli - command-line client for NetworkManager

SYNOPSIS
       nmcli [OPTIONS] OBJECT {COMMAND | help}

       OBJECT := general | networking | radio | connection | device

DESCRIPTION
       nmcli talks to NetworkManager, the service that manages the network on
       RHEL 9. With it you list network devices, and create, change, start
       and stop connection profiles. It is the main tool for network settings
       on the RHCSA exam.

       Two words matter. A device is a network interface, such as enp1s0. A
       connection (or connection profile) is a saved set of settings: the
       addresses, gateway, DNS servers and whether it starts at boot. A device
       uses one connection at a time. On RHEL 9 the profiles are kept as key
       files in /etc/NetworkManager/system-connections/NAME.nmconnection.

       Every object and command can be shortened, as long as it stays
       unambiguous: "nmcli con mod" is "nmcli connection modify", "nmcli d"
       is "nmcli device", "nmcli c s" is "nmcli connection show".

       Changing a profile with "connection modify" saves the new settings at
       once, but a running connection keeps its old settings until it is
       activated again: run "nmcli connection up NAME" (or reboot) afterwards.

OPTIONS
       -t, --terse
              Output for scripts: no headers, fields separated by colons.

       -f, --fields FIELDS
              Show only these fields (comma separated). With "connection show
              NAME" a field may be a whole setting, such as ipv4.

       -g, --get-values FIELDS
              Print only the values of these fields, one per line. Short for
              -t -f FIELDS without the field names. Good for tests in scripts.

       -p, --pretty
              Output for people, with headers and spacing.

       -a, --ask
              Ask for missing values (such as passwords) instead of failing.

       -w, --wait SECONDS
              How long to wait for an operation to finish.

COMMANDS
       general [status]
              The overall state of NetworkManager.

       general hostname [NAME]
              Show the static host name, or set it (written to /etc/hostname,
              as hostnamectl set-hostname does).

       networking [on | off | connectivity]
              Show, turn on or turn off all networking.

       device [status]
              List the devices, their type, state and the connection in use.

       device show [IFNAME]
              Details of a device: hardware address, IP addresses, gateway,
              DNS servers.

       device connect IFNAME, device disconnect IFNAME
              Activate the best connection for a device, or take it down.

       device reapply IFNAME
              Apply the changed settings of the current connection to the
              device without taking it down.

       connection show [--active] [NAME]
              List all profiles (or only the active ones). With a NAME, show
              every setting of that profile; lower-case names are the saved
              settings, upper-case ones (IP4.ADDRESS...) the live values.

       connection up NAME, connection down NAME
              Activate or deactivate a profile.

       connection add type TYPE [con-name NAME] [ifname IFNAME] [SETTINGS]
              Create a new profile and, if the device is free, start it.

       connection modify NAME [+|-]SETTING VALUE ...
              Change settings of a profile. A plain SETTING replaces the
              value, +SETTING adds to a list (another address or DNS server),
              -SETTING removes a value from a list.

       connection delete NAME
              Remove a profile (and take it down if it is active).

       connection reload
              Read the profile files again after editing them by hand.

SETTINGS
       The settings used most, with their short aliases for connection add:

       connection.id (con-name)
              The profile's name.

       connection.interface-name (ifname)
              The device the profile is for.

       connection.autoconnect (autoconnect) yes|no
              Start the profile at boot and when the device appears.

       ipv4.method auto|manual|disabled|link-local|shared
              auto = DHCP; manual = the addresses you give.

       ipv4.addresses (ip4) ADDRESS/PREFIX[,...]
              Static IPv4 addresses, for example 192.168.50.11/24. Always give
              the prefix: without it the address gets /32.

       ipv4.gateway (gw4) ADDRESS
              The default gateway.

       ipv4.dns ADDRESS[,...]
              DNS servers; NetworkManager writes them to /etc/resolv.conf.

       ipv4.dns-search DOMAIN[,...]
              Search domains for short host names.

       ipv4.ignore-auto-dns yes|no
              Ignore DNS servers handed out by DHCP.

       ipv6.method auto|manual|disabled|ignore|link-local|dhcp
       ipv6.addresses (ip6), ipv6.gateway (gw6), ipv6.dns
              The same for IPv6, for example 2001:db8::11/64.

EXAMPLES
       Look at the devices and profiles:

              # nmcli device status
              # nmcli connection show
              # nmcli -g ipv4.addresses,ipv4.gateway connection show enp1s0

       Give server1 a static address, gateway and DNS server, then apply it:

              # nmcli connection modify enp1s0 ipv4.method manual \
                  ipv4.addresses 192.168.50.11/24 ipv4.gateway 192.168.50.254 \
                  ipv4.dns 192.168.50.254 ipv4.dns-search lab.example.com
              # nmcli connection up enp1s0

       Add a second address and an IPv6 address to the same profile:

              # nmcli con mod enp1s0 +ipv4.addresses 192.168.50.111/24
              # nmcli con mod enp1s0 ipv6.method manual ipv6.addresses 2001:db8::11/64
              # nmcli con up enp1s0

       Create a new profile that starts at boot:

              # nmcli connection add con-name static1 type ethernet ifname enp1s0 \
                  ipv4.method manual ipv4.addresses 192.168.50.11/24 \
                  ipv4.gateway 192.168.50.254 autoconnect yes

       Set the host name:

              # nmcli general hostname server1.lab.example.com

FILES
       /etc/NetworkManager/system-connections/*.nmconnection
              The saved profiles (key files, mode 600, owned by root).

       /etc/resolv.conf
              Written by NetworkManager from the DNS settings.

NOTES
       Ordinary users may only look; changing settings needs root (or a user
       allowed by polkit). The text menu program nmtui changes the same
       profiles.

SEE ALSO
       nmtui(1), ip(8), hostnamectl(1), ping(8), ss(8)
